COURSE_DATA("intro",{"key":"intro","module":"INTRO","chapters":[{"id":"intro-0","title":"Course Overview","title_en":"Course Overview","intro":["Welcome to the English edition of “ISA 2027 for TISAX — Requirements Explained”. This course is based on the original English text of VDA ISA 2027.1 (released 2026-09-30). It explains the controls of the three modules Information Security, Prototype Protection and Data Protection one by one, and turns each requirement into management practices and audit preparation points that automotive suppliers can apply directly.","Maybe you are a quality manager who hears about TISAX for the first time. Maybe you are an information security officer who has run ISO 27001 for years. In both cases, this course takes you from “What does the requirement say?” to “How will the auditor look at it?” and finally to “What does our company need to prepare?”"],"blocks":[{"type":"h3","text":"Course objectives"},{"type":"list","items":["Understand how TISAX works: ENX registration, assessment scope, assessment levels (AL2 and AL3), assessment objectives and labels, finding categories and the corrective action process.","Read the structure of the ISA 2027.1 workbook and every column, and correctly tell apart **Must**, **Should**, **high protection needs**, **very high protection needs** and **SGA** requirements.","Master how maturity levels 0–5 are determined, understand why the target maturity level is 3 (Established), and how the cutback calculation works.","Understand the controls of Information Security chapters 1–7, Prototype Protection (chapter 8) and Data Protection (chapter 9) one by one, and map them to your company’s existing processes.","Plan an implementation roadmap, complete the ISA self-assessment, prepare assessment evidence and, after receiving findings, write an effective corrective action plan (CAP)."]},{"type":"h3","text":"Lecturer and assistant"},{"type":"table","head":["Role","Profile","Task in the course"],"rows":[["Lecturer Allan Lo","Consultant for ISMS and PIMS, and auditor at a certification body. Specialized in information security and corporate IT management. Has supported many automotive component, tooling and electronics suppliers.","Explains the requirements, shares real consulting and audit cases, and points out common findings."],["Assistant ARALE","A cheerful virtual assistant who asks questions on behalf of the learners, makes jokes and sums up the key points.","Hosts the sessions, asks questions, takes notes and leads the reviews."]]},{"type":"h3","text":"Course map"},{"type":"table","head":["Module","Content","Suggested time"],"rows":[["INTRO | TISAX overview","What TISAX is, relation to ISO 27001, participation process, assessment levels, assessment objectives and labels, finding categories, SGA","approx. 1 hour"],["INTRO | ISA structure and maturity","ISA 2027.1 tabs and columns, Must and Should, protection needs and CIA, maturity levels 0–5, cutback, KPIs, change history, license terms","approx. 1.5 hours"],["IS | Information Security chapters 1–7","Policies and organization, human resources, physical security, identity and access management, IT and network security, supplier relationships, compliance","approx. 8–10 hours"],["PP | Prototype Protection (chapter 8)","8.1 Organizational requirements, 8.2 Physical and environmental security","approx. 2 hours"],["DP | Data Protection (chapter 9)","Yes/no controls for processing on behalf of a controller according to Art. 28 GDPR","approx. 1 hour"],["AUDIT | Audit practice","Implementation roadmap, document checklist, how to handle the assessment day, top 30 common findings and the CAP","approx. 2 hours"],["Final test and certificate","After passing the online test, you can download a certificate of completion","approx. 0.5 hours"]]},{"type":"h3","text":"How to learn with this course"},{"type":"olist","items":["Finish the two INTRO chapters first. They build a common language for “how TISAX works” and “how to read the ISA”. Without them, the controls are hard to follow.","After each control, open your company’s own ISA self-assessment and write down “how we do it today” and “where the evidence is”.","You can review the videos at 1.25x speed, but we recommend normal speed for the sections on the maturity model and on Must and Should.","Take the “typical auditor questions” of each chapter and ask the responsible colleagues. This works like a mock interview before the real assessment.","When something is unclear, go back to the original ISA columns (Objective, Requirements, Examples) and read them again. Do not rely only on summaries."]},{"type":"tip","who":"allan","text":"The situation I see most often: a company treats TISAX as a “fill in one Excel sheet” project. Then the auditor says “Please show me the records” and nobody can answer. Take this course and collect evidence at the same time. That works best."},{"type":"tip","who":"arale","text":"Note this down! The “Key points” at the end of each chapter can go straight into your internal training slides."},{"type":"callout","style":"info","title":"Version note","text":"This course is based on VDA ISA 2027.1 (2026-09-30). For TISAX assessment rules (for example the mapping of assessment objectives to labels, validity periods and process details), the latest ENX TISAX Participant Handbook and the announcements on the official ENX website apply. Please check them again before you register for an assessment."}],"controls":[],"summary":["This course is based on the original English text of VDA ISA 2027.1 and covers the three modules Information Security, Prototype Protection and Data Protection.","Course map: TISAX overview → ISA structure and maturity → Information Security chapters 1–7 → Prototype Protection → Data Protection → Audit practice → Final test and certificate.","Keep your company’s self-assessment open while you learn, and collect evidence as you go.","For details of the assessment rules, the latest ENX Participant Handbook applies."]},{"id":"intro-1","title":"What Is TISAX","title_en":"What is TISAX","intro":["TISAX (Trusted Information Security Assessment Exchange) is the information security assessment and exchange mechanism used across the European automotive industry. The idea is simple: instead of every car maker sending its own auditors to the same supplier, the industry uses one common catalog (VDA ISA), one common set of assessment rules and accredited audit providers. The supplier is assessed once, and the result is shared with the customers who need it through the ENX platform.","This chapter explains the background of TISAX, how it differs from and relates to ISO 27001, the full participant process from registration to label, assessment levels and assessment methods, assessment objectives and labels, finding categories, and the Simplified Group Assessment (SGA) for organizations with many sites."],"blocks":[{"type":"h3","text":"1. Background: VDA, ENX and ISA"},{"type":"p","text":"**VDA** (Verband der Automobilindustrie, German Association of the Automotive Industry) has long driven information security requirements in the automotive industry. The **VDA ISA** (Information Security Assessment) is the information security catalog developed by industry working groups. It is based on the controls of ISO/IEC 27001 and adds requirements specific to the automotive industry for prototype protection and data protection. According to the License tab of ISA 2027.1, the ISA is a “work of ENX’s Working Group ISA” that was “provided to the VDA … by the **ENX Association** … published by the VDA as the VDA ISA”. The Data Protection tab is “provided, owned and copyrighted by” the VDA."},{"type":"p","text":"The **ENX Association** is an organization of European car manufacturers, suppliers and industry associations. It operates the TISAX mechanism: it manages participant registration, accredits audit providers, maintains the assessment rules (the TISAX Participant Handbook and other documents) and runs the ENX Portal, where participants share their assessment results."},{"type":"p","text":"The Welcome tab of the ISA states that “ISA provides the basis for” three uses: (1) “a self-assessment to determine the state of information security in an organization”; (2) “audits performed by internal departments (e.g., Internal Audit, Information Security)”; (3) “TISAX Assessments”. In other words, the ISA itself is a public catalog, and TISAX is the “assessment and exchange mechanism” that uses this catalog."},{"type":"h3","text":"2. TISAX and ISO 27001: differences and relationship"},{"type":"table","head":["Aspect","ISO/IEC 27001","TISAX"],"rows":[["Nature","International management system standard; a certification body issues a “certificate”","Assessment and exchange mechanism of the automotive industry; you receive a “label”, not a certificate"],["Basis of review","ISO/IEC 27001:2022 clauses 4–10 and the Annex A controls","VDA ISA (Information Security + Prototype Protection + Data Protection); controls are written as questions and rated by maturity level"],["How results are determined","Conformity or non-conformity (based on the clauses and the Statement of Applicability, SoA)","Each control is rated at maturity level 0–5 with a target level of 3; findings are based on the Must, Should, high and very high requirements"],["Scope","The organization defines the ISMS scope","The TISAX assessment scope (in practice usually the standard scope defined by ENX) and the selected assessment objectives decide which tabs and requirements are assessed"],["Publication of results","Certificates can be checked publicly (depending on the certification body’s rules)","Results are stored in the ENX Portal; the assessed organization decides which customers see them and at which level of detail"],["Industry-specific requirements","None","Prototype protection (physical security, camouflage, test drives, handling of images), data protection, automotive customer networks and passing on customer requirements"]]},{"type":"p","text":"You can see the relationship like this: the controls of the ISA Information Security tab are based on ISO/IEC 27001, and every control in ISA 2027.1 lists the matching ISO 27001:2022 Annex A reference. A company with ISO 27001 usually already has the backbone of an ISMS: risk assessment, internal audit and management review. But **an ISO 27001 certificate does not automatically give you a TISAX label**. The gaps usually appear in three areas: the evidence of a “standardized and continuously operated” process needed for maturity level 3; ISA-specific details (for example approval of external IT services, approval of software, the life cycle of identification means, and the physical requirements of prototype protection); and the additional requirements for high and very high protection needs."},{"type":"tip","who":"allan","text":"I once supported a tool-and-die maker that had held ISO 27001 for three years. In the self-assessment they confidently entered a whole column of 3s. Then we did the gap analysis: for just three controls, 1.3.3 external IT services, 1.3.4 software approval and 4.1.1 identification means, there were no records at all. ISO is a good foundation, but TISAX asks in more detail."},{"type":"h3","text":"3. Participant process (from registration to sharing the result)"},{"type":"olist","items":["**ENX registration**: register as a participant in the ENX Portal, enter company data and contact persons, and accept the TISAX participation terms. You need company identification data for the registration (the ISA Cover tab also has a field for the D-U-N-S number).","**Define the assessment scope and assessment objectives**: choose the locations to be assessed and the assessment objectives (for example Info high, Proto parts, Data). The assessment objectives usually depend on customer requirements. First ask your customer’s purchasing or information security contact which labels they need.","**Select an audit provider**: choose one of the accredited audit providers published by ENX, sign a contract and agree on the assessment dates. The cost depends on the number of locations, the assessment objectives and the assessment level. Ask the audit provider directly for a quote.","**ISA self-assessment**: complete the self-assessment with the latest ISA version. For each control, enter the maturity level, an implementation description and the reference documentation, and submit it to the audit provider before the assessment.","**Assessment (initial assessment)**: depending on the assessment level, the auditor reviews documents, conducts interviews and (for AL3) inspects on site. At the end, the auditor issues an assessment report and a list of findings.","**Corrective Action Plan (CAP)**: for each finding, define the root cause, measures, deadline and responsible person. The audit provider reviews and accepts the plan.","**Corrective action assessment (follow-up)**: the auditor confirms that the corrective actions are implemented and effective. Depending on the type of finding, this can be a document review or another on-site visit.","**Receive the label and share it in the ENX Portal**: after closure, the TISAX label is created in the ENX Portal. The assessed organization decides which customers (participants) it shares the result with.","**Validity and re-assessment**: a TISAX label is generally valid for three years. Before it expires, you need to plan a re-assessment. If the scope or the assessment objectives change in the meantime, an additional assessment may be needed."]},{"type":"callout","style":"warn","title":"Do not rely only on the time frames in this course","text":"The actual deadlines of each phase (for example how long you have to complete corrective actions, or how long a temporary label is valid) change when the ENX rules are updated. This course only describes the general situation. For your planning, the latest ENX TISAX Participant Handbook and the information from your audit provider apply."},{"type":"h3","text":"4. Assessment levels and assessment methods"},{"type":"table","head":["Assessment level","Typical approach","Typical use"],"rows":[["AL1","Self-assessment only, without verification by an independent audit provider. Low credibility; in practice TISAX does not issue labels based on AL1.","Internal self-checks, or when a customer only asks for a completed self-assessment questionnaire."],["AL2","The auditor performs a **plausibility check** of the self-assessment: reviews documents and evidence and confirms them in interviews. In practice this is mostly done remotely (video conference + document sharing); an on-site visit is possible if needed or requested by the assessed organization.","Information with high protection needs (Info high), high availability (Availability high), data protection (Data), etc."],["AL3","The auditor performs a **thorough verification**: in addition to documents and interviews, usually an on-site inspection, observation of real operations, and sample checks of records and system settings.","Very high protection needs (Info very high), all prototype protection objectives, special categories of personal data (Special data), etc."]]},{"type":"p","text":"An easy way to remember it: **AL2 checks “Is what you say plausible, and is there evidence?” AL3 checks “Is what you do real, and does the site really look like this?”** An AL3 auditor will ask you to open system screens, walk to the server room and the prototype areas, and ask employees questions on the spot."},{"type":"h3","text":"5. Assessment objectives and labels (common mapping)"},{"type":"p","text":"TISAX participants choose “assessment objectives” at registration and receive the matching “labels” after a successful assessment. The table below shows the mapping commonly used in the industry. **The actual objective names, the corresponding assessment levels and the ISA controls in scope are defined in the latest ENX TISAX Participant Handbook** (ISA 2027 has rearranged the prototype protection controls, so the mapping may change accordingly)."},{"type":"table","head":["Assessment objective (common name)","Common label name","Typical assessment level","Main coverage"],"rows":[["Info high","Confidential","AL2","Information Security tab (including confidentiality requirements for high protection needs)"],["Info very high","Strictly confidential","AL3","Information Security tab (including confidentiality requirements for very high protection needs)"],["Availability high","High availability","AL2","Information Security tab (including availability requirements for high protection needs)"],["Availability very high","Very high availability","AL3","Information Security tab (including availability requirements for very high protection needs)"],["Proto parts","Proto parts","AL3","Prototype protection: parts and components"],["Proto vehicles","Proto vehicles","AL3","Prototype protection: prototype vehicles"],["Test vehicles","Test vehicles","AL3","Prototype protection: use of test vehicles (e.g., road tests, test tracks)"],["Proto events","Proto events","AL3","Prototype protection: events such as presentations and photo or film shootings"],["Data","Data","AL2","Data Protection tab (processing of personal data on behalf of a controller according to Art. 28 GDPR)"],["Special data","Special data","AL3","Data Protection tab (involving special categories of personal data)"]]},{"type":"tip","who":"arale","text":"I see! So we do not decide ourselves which label we want. It depends on what the customer asks for. Ask the customer first, then register, so you do not waste money."},{"type":"tip","who":"allan","text":"Almost all prototype objectives need AL3, and the auditor will definitely come on site to look at fences, access control, video surveillance and screening. Some companies needed several months just to put privacy film on the windows of the prototype area and upgrade the access control system. Plan early."},{"type":"h3","text":"6. The concept of finding categories"},{"type":"table","head":["Category","Concept","Impact on the label (general case)"],"rows":[["Major non-conformity","The control objective is not achieved, or Must requirements are clearly not implemented, so there is a significant information security risk. It can also be a systematic or widespread gap.","It must be corrected and confirmed by the audit provider before a regular label can be issued. Usually no temporary label is issued first."],["Minor non-conformity","The control generally works, but some requirements are not fully met, or the evidence is not enough to show continuous operation. The risk is limited.","After an acceptable corrective action plan is submitted, a temporary label is often possible first; the corrections are then completed within the deadline."],["Observation","Currently compliant, but there is a potential risk or trend to watch. If not handled, it may become a non-conformity in the future.","No impact on the label; it should be included in improvement activities."],["Room for improvement","The requirements are met, but the auditor sees a better approach that could raise maturity or efficiency.","No impact on the label; it is a recommendation."]]},{"type":"p","text":"**Temporary label**: if the assessment shows no major non-conformities, only minor non-conformities, and the audit provider has accepted the corrective action plan, the participant can usually receive a temporary label with a shorter validity first. This tells customers that you are “in the process of correcting, and trustworthy overall”. After the corrective action assessment is completed, the temporary label is replaced by the regular label. For the conditions and validity of temporary labels, the latest ENX rules apply."},{"type":"h3","text":"7. Simplified Group Assessment (SGA)"},{"type":"p","text":"The ISA Definitions tab defines SGA as **“A simplified way to audit very large organizations with a high maturity. An example is the TISAX Simplified Group Assessment mechanism that is an option for TISAX Assessments of an assessment scope with a large number of sites.”** In practice, the idea of SGA is: the group manages all sites with one centrally managed, highly standardized ISMS. The audit provider first fully assesses the headquarters and the central processes, and then assesses only a sample of sites, instead of fully assessing every single site."},{"type":"p","text":"For this reason, the Information Security tab of ISA 2027.1 has a column **“Additional requirements for Simplified Group Assessments (SGA)”**. Examples: 1.1.1 “Policies are published and implemented in the entire assessment scope.”; 1.2.1 “The management system is approved by an entity that has the necessary authority for the entire assessment scope (i.e., all locations within the scope).”; 1.2.2 “A named person with overall responsibility for the management system exists and is available.” Only organizations that use SGA need to meet this column. For SGA eligibility (for example number of sites, degree of central management, previous assessment results), the latest ENX rules apply."},{"type":"callout","style":"ok","title":"This chapter in one sentence","text":"TISAX is the automotive industry’s “assess once, use many times” information security assessment and exchange mechanism: VDA ISA is the catalog, accredited audit providers assess at AL2 or AL3, and the result is shared as a label in the ENX Portal, generally valid for three years."}],"controls":[],"summary":["VDA ISA is the catalog; TISAX is the assessment and exchange mechanism operated by ENX that uses the ISA. The ISA can also be used for self-assessments and internal audits.","ISO 27001 is a good foundation but is not the same as TISAX. Gaps often appear in the evidence of continuous operation for maturity level 3, in ISA-specific details and in the high and very high requirements.","Participant process: ENX registration → define scope and assessment objectives → select an audit provider → ISA self-assessment → assessment → corrective action plan → confirmation of corrections → receive the label and share it in the ENX Portal. A label is generally valid for three years.","AL2 is a plausibility check based on documents and interviews, mostly remote; AL3 is a thorough verification, mainly on site.","Common assessment objectives and labels: Info high / Confidential, Info very high / Strictly confidential, Availability high / very high, Proto parts, Proto vehicles, Test vehicles, Proto events, Data, Special data. The latest Participant Handbook defines the actual mapping.","Findings are classified as major non-conformity, minor non-conformity, observation and room for improvement. If there are only minor non-conformities and the CAP is accepted, a temporary label is usually possible first.","SGA is for organizations with many sites, central management and high maturity. The ISA has a separate column for SGA requirements."]},{"id":"intro-2","title":"Structure and Interpretation of ISA 2027","title_en":"Structure and Interpretation of ISA 2027","intro":["The ISA is an Excel workbook. When people open it for the first time, many are scared by the dense columns. But once you understand which tabs exist, what each column means, how Must differs from Should and how maturity is rated, you can read every control with the same method.","Based on the original text of ISA 2027.1 (2026-09-30), this chapter explains the workbook structure, the meaning of the columns, protection needs and the CIA markers, the maturity model 0–5 and the cutback calculation, KPI examples, the main changes in ISA 2027, and the key points of the license terms."],"blocks":[{"type":"h3","text":"1. Structure of the workbook (tabs)"},{"type":"p","text":"The Welcome tab recommends: “We recommend gaining an overview of the individual ISA tabs by using the ‘Definitions’ tab. Then, commence with the ‘Information Security’ tab.” The tabs, based on the descriptions in the Definitions tab, are summarized below:"},{"type":"table","head":["Tab","Content","Intended use of tab"],"rows":[["Welcome","Welcome page: explains that the ISA provides the basis for self-assessments, internal audits and TISAX Assessments. 2027.1 added a feedback link (ENX ISA-Feedback).","Information"],["Cover","“The cover contains boxes for information on the implementing organization, the scope of review, the auditor and the contact person of the organization under review”: organization, address, Scope / TISAX Scope ID, D-U-N-S number, assessment date, contact person, author and signature; version label 2027.1 | 2026-09-30.","To be completed by the organization"],["Maturity levels","Name, principle, definition (including Process Attributes) and possible evidence for maturity levels 0–5. “With this VDA ISA version, the target maturity level for all control questions is consistently 3 (Established).”","Information"],["Definitions","Description of the tabs, key terms (protection classes, Must and Should, high and very high requirements, “The following aspects are considered”, protection goals) and a glossary.","Information"],["Information Security","“All basic controls based on the standard ISO/IEC 27001” (chapters 1–7). “The controls themselves are formulated as questions” and are rated by maturity level.","Implementation requirements"],["Prototype Protection","Prototype protection controls (chapter 8: 8.1 Organizational requirements, 8.2 Physical and environmental security), rated by maturity level.","Implementation requirements"],["Data Protection","“To be edited additionally in case of processing within the meaning of Art. 28 of the EU General Data Protection Regulation and contains controls requiring merely yes/no answers” (chapter 9).","Implementation requirements"],["Results","Summary of the results of all tabs, with the spider web diagram, results per chapter and per subchapter, and the overall result with cutback.","Presentation of results"],["Examples KPI","Examples of KPIs to support “identifying own suitable KPIs”. “It does not present mandatory requirements for achieving maturity level 4.”","Examples and support"],["License","“License conditions under which the VDA ISA is published”: CC BY-ND 4.0 plus special terms for distributing derivatives.","Information"],["Change history","“List of changes over the VDA ISA lifecycle.”","Information"]]},{"type":"callout","style":"info","title":"Important note on what is assessed","text":"The original text clearly states: “Requirements always refer to the own company with respect to its organization, processes and infrastructure. **Requirements never refer to products put on the market by your company.** Requirements for secure product development are not part of this module.” In other words, the ISA does not audit whether your ECU software has Secure Boot. It audits how your company protects information."},{"type":"h3","text":"2. Control columns (example: Information Security tab)"},{"type":"table","head":["Column","Meaning and how to read it"],"rows":[["Control question","A question that starts with “To what extent…”. The answer is not yes or no but a maturity level."],["Objective","What the control wants to achieve. In the end, the auditor judges whether the objective is achieved; the requirements are only the minimum conditions to achieve it."],["Requirements (must)","“Strict requirements without any exemptions.”"],["Requirements (should)","In principle they must be implemented. With a valid justification they may not be met, but the organization must understand the effects of the deviation and justify it plausibly."],["Additional requirements for high protection needs","Must additionally be met if the assessed scope has high protection needs. The relevant protection goals are marked with (C, I, A) at the end of the requirement."],["Additional requirements for very high protection needs","Must additionally be met if the assessed scope has very high protection needs; also marked with (C, I, A)."],["Additional requirements for SGA","Apply only to organizations that use the Simplified Group Assessment."],["Reference to other standards","References to ISO 27001:2022 Annex A, ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 and NIST CSF 2.0, to help organizations that already use other standards."],["Implementation guidance","References for implementation, for example BSI-Standard 200-2, modules of the BSI IT-Grundschutz-Compendium and NIST SP800-53r5 controls."],["Supplementary information / Examples","Supplementary information (Introduction, Justification, Basic information) and implementation examples for normal, high and very high protection needs."],["Possible questions","Questions the auditor may ask. This is the best question bank for interview preparation."],["Possible evidence","Possible evidence, for example policies, records, system screens. This is the starting point for your evidence list."],["Maturity level","Enter 0–5 or na in the self-assessment or assessment; the value is transferred automatically to the Results tab."]]},{"type":"tip","who":"allan","text":"Here is how I teach people to read a control: first read the Objective, then the Must, then the Possible questions. After these three columns, you know what yardstick the auditor has in mind. The Examples are a reference, not the one correct answer."},{"type":"h3","text":"3. Definitions of Must and Should"},{"type":"table","head":["Type","Original definition","Practical interpretation"],"rows":[["Must","“The requirements indicated in this column are strict requirements without any exemptions.”","If one item is missing, it can be rated as a non-conformity. There is no room for negotiation."],["Should","“Principally, the requirements indicated in this column must be implemented by the organization. In certain circumstances, however, there may be a valid justification for non-compliance with these requirements. In case of any deviation, its effects must be understood by the organization and it must be plausibly justified.”","It does not mean “optional”. It means “if you do not do it, you need a written, plausible and assessed justification”."],["Additional requirements for high protection needs","“The requirements indicated in this column must additionally be met if the assessed scope has high protection needs.”","Applies when you select objectives such as Info high or Availability high."],["Additional requirements for very high protection needs","“The requirements indicated in this column must additionally be met if the assessed scope has very high protection needs.”","Applies when you select objectives such as Info very high or Availability very high; the high protection needs requirements must also be met."]]},{"type":"callout","style":"warn","title":"Common misunderstanding","text":"Many people read Should as “recommended” and skip the whole column in the self-assessment. According to the ISA definition, Should means “principally must”. The auditor will ask: “You did not do this one. What is the justification? Where is the impact assessment? Who approved it?” If you cannot answer, it is a finding."},{"type":"h3","text":"4. Protection needs normal / high / very high and the CIA markers"},{"type":"table","head":["Protection class","Original explanation","Examples"],"rows":[["normal","“The potential damage to the organization is limited and manageable.”","Confidentiality classification “internal”"],["high","“The potential damage to the organization may be substantial.”","Confidentiality classification “confidential”, or high availability"],["very high","“The potential damage can reach a level of potentially threatening or disastrous impact on the organization’s existence.”","Confidentiality classification “strictly confidential”, or very high availability"]]},{"type":"p","text":"The additional requirements for high and very high protection needs are marked by protection goal: **C = Confidentiality, I = Integrity, A = Availability**. For example, the high protection needs requirement of 5.1.2 “Information is transported or transferred in encrypted form (at least transport-encryption) or protected by similarly effective measures. (C)” concerns only confidentiality. The very high protection needs requirement of 5.2.9 “(Additional) Backups are performed via offline procedures, immutable backups or by using an isolated IAM solution. (I, A)” concerns integrity and availability. In practice, if your assessment objective is Availability high, pay special attention to the additional requirements marked A; for Info high, focus on C (and related I). Which requirements actually apply depends on the definition of the assessment objectives in the Participant Handbook."},{"type":"h3","text":"5. The meaning of “The following aspects are considered”"},{"type":"p","text":"Many ISA requirements continue with “The following aspects are considered:” and then list several sub-items. The Definitions tab defines this phrase clearly: **“The phrase ‘the following aspects are considered’ means that, for each listed aspect, a conscious and reasoned decision should have been made as to whether, to what extent, and how a measure is implemented. In an audit situation, the auditee should be able to explain the underlying reasoning and the resulting decision.”**"},{"type":"p","text":"In other words, these sub-items are not a list of things you must “all do”, but a list of things you must “all think through”. For example, the Must of 2.1.4 mobile work lists “Behavior in private surroundings (e.g., working in a dedicated office at home or in a coworking space)”, “Behavior in public surroundings”, “Secure handling of and access to information … while considering the protection needs and the contractual requirements”, “Protection from theft in public surroundings” and “Measures to ensure a secure access to organization’s IT-services”. You can decide to cover one item with a technical control and another with rules and awareness, but every item needs a decision and a reason."},{"type":"tip","who":"arale","text":"Note this down! When you see “The following aspects are considered”, add a small table next to your self-assessment: aspect / our decision / reason / evidence."},{"type":"h3","text":"6. The maturity model 0–5"},{"type":"p","text":"The original text says: “The answer to the control questions is a maturity level in a generic maturity model used to quantify the maturity of the corresponding processes. Determination of the maturity level requires that **objective evidence** of compliance with the requirements of the respective level is provided during the assessment. This is achieved, for example, by means of work products resulting from the processes of the control questions or by means of interview statements by persons carrying out the process.” The model is based on the concepts of SPICE (ISO/IEC 33001)."},{"type":"table","head":["Level","Name","Principle (original)","Possible evidence (GWP)"],"rows":[["0","Incomplete","A process does not exist, is not followed or not suitable to achieve the objective. There is little or no evidence of systematic process performance.","—"],["1","Performed","A process is followed which is not or insufficiently documented (“informal process”) and there is some evidence that it achieves its objective. The implemented process achieves its (process) purpose; the intended base practices are verifiably performed.","Work products providing evidence of process outcomes"],["2","Managed","A process achieving its objectives is followed. Process documentation and process implementation evidence are available.","Process documentation, process plan, quality plan/records, process implementation records"],["3","Established","A standard process integrated into the overall system is followed. Dependencies on other processes are documented and suitable interfaces are created. Evidence exists that the process has been used sustainably and actively over an extended period.","Process documentation, process plan, quality records, policies and standards, process implementation records"],["4","Predictable","An established process is followed. The effectiveness of the process is continually monitored by collecting key figures. Limit values are defined at which the process is considered to be insufficiently effective and requires adjustment (Key Performance Indicators).","Process documentation, process control plan, process improvement plan, process measurement plan, process implementation records"],["5","Optimizing","A predictable process with continual improvement as a major objective is followed. Improvement is actively advanced by means of dedicated resources.","Process improvement plan, process measurement plan, process implementation records"]]},{"type":"p","text":"The Process Attributes (PA) of each level are as follows:"},{"type":"table","head":["Level","Process attribute","Key content (condensed from the original)"],"rows":[["2","PA 2.1 Control of process implementation","Objectives for the performance of the process are identified; implementation is planned, monitored and adjusted to meet plans; responsibilities and authorities are defined, assigned and communicated; necessary resources and information are identified, made available, assigned and used; interfaces between the involved parties are managed to ensure effective communication and clear assignment of responsibilities."],["2","PA 2.2 Work Product Management","Requirements for the work products are defined; requirements for documentation and control of the work products are defined; work products are appropriately identified, documented and controlled; work products are reviewed in accordance with planned measures and adjusted as necessary."],["3","PA 3.1 Process Definition","A standard process, including appropriately adapted requirements, is defined; the sequence and interaction of the standard process with other processes are determined; required competencies and roles are identified; required infrastructure and work environment are identified; suitable methods for monitoring the effectiveness and suitability of the process are determined."],["3","PA 3.2 Process Deployment","A defined process based on an appropriately selected and/or tailored standard process is deployed; roles, responsibilities and authorities are assigned and communicated; personnel are competent on the basis of appropriate education, training and experience; necessary resources and information are made available, allocated and used; necessary infrastructure and work environment are available, managed and maintained; appropriate data is collected and analysed to understand the behaviour of the process, demonstrate its suitability and effectiveness, and evaluate where continuous improvement can be made."],["4","PA 4.1 Process Measurement","Process information requirements in support of business goals are established; process measurement objectives and quantitative objectives for process performance are derived; characteristic values and frequency of measurements are defined; results of measurement are collected, analysed and reported; measurement results are used to characterize process performance."],["4","PA 4.2 Process control","Analysis and control techniques are determined and applied; variable control limits are established for normal process implementation; measurement data is analysed for special variations; corrective actions are taken to address special variations; control limits are re-established as necessary."],["5","PA 5.1 Process Innovation","Process improvement objectives are defined; data are analysed to identify common causes of variations; data are analysed to identify options for best practice and innovation; improvement options from new technologies and new process concepts are identified; an implementation strategy is established to achieve the improvement objectives."],["5","PA 5.2 Continuous optimization","The impact of all proposed changes is assessed against the objectives of the defined process and the standard process; implementation of all agreed changes is managed so that any disruption to process performance is understood and addressed; based on actual performance, the effectiveness of process change is evaluated."]]},{"type":"callout","style":"ok","title":"Target maturity level = 3","text":"The Definitions tab clearly states: “With this VDA ISA version, the target maturity level for all control questions is consistently 3 (Established).” So you must show more than “we do it” (1) and “we have documents and records” (2). You must show “we have a standard process, it is integrated with other processes, and it has been running continuously for some time” (3)."},{"type":"h3","text":"7. Results and the cutback calculation"},{"type":"p","text":"The Results tab summarizes the results of all controls automatically. The original text says: **“When calculating the overall result, the results of controls overachieving their target maturity level are cutback and averaged. This ensures that the requirements are comprehensively fulfilled and that there is no compensation of overachieved and underachieved controls.”** In addition, “the line for the target maturity level does not consider controls marked ‘N/A’. When calculating the average, however, the maximum achievable target maturity level is taken into account.” The results per chapter and per subchapter are shown “without cutback”."},{"type":"table","head":["Control","Assessment result","After cutback"],"rows":[["1.1.1","4","3"],["1.2.1","3","3"],["1.2.2","2","2"],["1.3.1","5","3"],["1.3.2","3","3"],["Average","Without cutback: 17 ÷ 5 = 3.4","With cutback: 14 ÷ 5 = 2.8"]]},{"type":"p","text":"This example shows: without cutback, the average of 3.4 looks above target. After cutback it is only 2.8, which correctly shows that 1.2.2 does not reach the target. The maximum score is 3. In the industry you sometimes hear that “the total score must reach a certain threshold”, but whether TISAX issues a label depends mainly on the auditor’s findings for individual controls and on the corrective actions. Do not focus only on the average score."},{"type":"tip","who":"allan","text":"The sentence I hear most often during audits is: “We are at level 4 for this one!” I always smile and say: thank you, but the system will cut you back to 3, and I still need to see what you do about that other control at 2."},{"type":"h3","text":"8. KPI examples (Examples KPI)"},{"type":"p","text":"The Examples KPI tab gives KPI examples for many controls. Each KPI belongs to one of two types, COVERAGE and EFFECTIVENESS, and describes the objective (vision), recipients, frequency, threshold levels, measurement, interfaces and components. The original text stresses that the tab “**does not present mandatory requirements for achieving maturity level 4**. Definition of KPIs is not mandatory, but may be helpful for a central management of information security at many locations.” Here are some commonly used examples:"},{"type":"table","head":["Control","KPI name","Type","Measurement (original)","Example threshold"],"rows":[["2.1.3","Coverage degree of awareness measures","COVERAGE","Quotient: number of participants / total number of employees","To be determined individually, e.g., Green > 90%, Yellow 70–90%, Red < 70%"],["2.1.3","Effectiveness of awareness measures","EFFECTIVENESS","Determining the number of security incidents with human error as a cause","E.g., 0–20 low, 20–50 medium, 50+ high; for comparability, e.g., incidents per 100 employees"],["4.1.3","Coverage degree review “User accounts”","COVERAGE","Quotient: number of performed reviews / total number of IT systems in scope","E.g., Green > 90%, Yellow 70–90%, Red < 70%"],["4.1.3","“Collective accounts”","EFFECTIVENESS","Determining the number of collective accounts (adjusted for authorized exceptions)","Red > 0, Green = 0"],["5.2.1","Change - error rate","EFFECTIVENESS","Quotient: number of reversed changes / total number of performed changes","E.g., Green < 10%, Yellow 10–30%, Red > 30%"],["5.2.3","Coverage degree Endpoint Security","COVERAGE","Quotient: number of protected IT systems / total number of IT systems (adjusted for authorized exceptions)","E.g., Green > 90%, Yellow 70–90%, Red < 70%"],["5.2.5","Effectiveness of patch installation","EFFECTIVENESS","Time comparison: average actual rollout state vs. target state","E.g., target: 100% after max. 10 days"],["5.2.9","Coverage degree of restoration tests","COVERAGE","Quotient: number of IT systems with tested restoration from backup / total number of all backed-up IT systems","E.g., Green > 90%, Yellow 70–90%, Red < 70%"],["1.1.1","Actuality of required policies/documentations","COVERAGE","Quotient: number of policies reviewed according to cycle / population of policies to be reviewed","Target coverage = 100%"]]},{"type":"tip","who":"arale","text":"Wow, so KPIs do not mean we have to build dozens at once. Pick the 3 to 5 most meaningful ones, for example user account reviews, patching and backup restore tests, and track them every quarter in the management review report. That already helps a lot."},{"type":"h3","text":"9. Main changes in ISA 2027 (according to the Change history)"},{"type":"p","text":"Starting with ISA 2027, versions are named by year. The table below is based on the “2027” and “2027.1” entries in the Change history:"},{"type":"table","head":["Version","Area","Change"],"rows":[["2027","General","Changed naming convention according to the year (now 2027)."],["2027","General","Translation corrections and some rewordings for better understanding."],["2027","General","NIST CSF 2.0 mapping added; ISO 27001:2013 mappings removed."],["2027","General","Column “O” in the Information Security tab and column “M” in the Prototype Protection tab, “Usual person responsible for process implementation”, deleted."],["2027","Information Security 1.1.1","Requirement (should) changed to Requirement (must): “The policies are made available to employees in a suitable form (e.g., intranet).”"],["2027","Information Security 1.2.4 → 6.1.3","Control-ID 1.2.4 was changed to 6.1.3 (responsibilities between external IT service providers and the own organization)."],["2027","Information Security 2.1.3","Added specific training programs for senior management/executive leadership."],["2027","Information Security 2.1.4","Reformulation of Requirement (must)."],["2027","Information Security 4.1.1","Requirement for high protection needs: time limitation of identification means has been deleted."],["2027","Information Security 4.1.2","Requirement (should): minus changed to plus (password according to the state of the art), so it became a formal requirement item."],["2027","Information Security 5.2.3","Requirement (should): deletion of “Case-related staff awareness measures” (reason: covered by 2.1.3)."],["2027","Information Security 5.2.8","Requirements for high protection needs: “-” changed to “+”; very high protection needs: “Tests and any lessons learned are documented. (I, A)” changed to “Results and any lessons learned are recorded. (I, A)”."],["2027","Information Security 5.3.1","Control question: “IT system” changed to “IT service”."],["2027","Information Security 6.1.1","Requirement (must) “Compliance with contractual agreements will be verified.” moved to requirements for high protection needs; new requirements defined for very high protection needs."],["2027","Prototype Protection (general)","All controls for prototype protection have been rearranged; controls 8.3–8.5 deleted; column “L” deleted."],["2027","Prototype Protection (specific)","8.1 Physical and Environmental Security changed to 8.2 with all associated controls except 8.1.7; 8.1.7 moved to 8.1.8; 8.2 Organizational Requirements changed to 8.1, with new controls 8.1.1, 8.1.2, 8.1.4, 8.1.11, 8.1.12, 8.1.13 and deleted control 8.2.4."],["2027.1","General","Corrections of spelling and punctuation errors."],["2027.1","Welcome","Feedback URL added."],["2027.1","Definitions","Definitions for “coworking space” and “office-less” added; revised definition of “project”."],["2027.1","Information Security 4.1.2","“State-of-the-art” phrasing replaced."],["2027.1","Prototype Protection 8.1.1","Should requirement removed."],["2027.1","Prototype Protection 8.1.8","Protection against unauthorized access to security areas: “including traceable documentation” added."],["2027.1","Results","Errors in the calculation and the spider chart have been corrected."],["2027.1","Change history","6.1.3 Must requirement: Supplier SOA removed; the 2027 change of control 2.1.3 added to the record."]]},{"type":"callout","style":"info","title":"Version transition reminder","text":"If your self-assessment or internal documents still say 1.2.4, change it to 6.1.3. The prototype protection numbering has been completely rearranged, so check all your old mapping tables again."},{"type":"h3","text":"10. Key points of the license terms"},{"type":"list","items":["The ISA is licensed under **Creative Commons Attribution - No Derivative Works 4.0 International (CC BY-ND 4.0)**. You are free to “copy and redistribute the material in any medium or format for any purpose, even commercially.”","**Attribution**: “You must give appropriate credit, provide a link to the license, and indicate if changes were made. You may do so in any reasonable manner, but not in any way that suggests the licensor endorses you or your use.”","**Distribution of derivatives (Section 9, additional rights not part of the Creative Commons license)**: if you change or build upon the material, you may distribute the modified material only if (1) it is “clearly marked as **a modified version not approved by the Licensor**”; (2) “the license terms have not been modified”; and (3) “**any logos and/or trademarks of the Licensor have been removed**”.","**No additional restrictions**: “You may not apply any additional legal terms or technological measures that legally restrict others from doing anything the license permits.”","Copyright: © 2026 ENX Association; the Data Protection tab is “provided, owned and copyrighted by” the VDA."]},{"type":"callout","style":"warn","title":"Statement of this course","text":"This course contains excerpts, summaries and explanations of ISA 2027.1. It is a modified version not approved by the ENX Association or the VDA and does not represent the views of the licensors. The original English text of the ISA is authoritative for all formal requirements."}],"controls":[],"summary":["The ISA consists of the tabs Welcome, Cover, Maturity levels, Definitions, Information Security, Prototype Protection, Data Protection, Results, Examples KPI, License and Change history.","Order for reading a control: Objective → Must → Should → high / very high → Possible questions → Possible evidence.","Must has no exemptions; Should must principally be implemented, and any deviation must be understood and plausibly justified.","Protection needs are normal, high and very high; additional requirements are marked C, I or A for the related protection goal.","“The following aspects are considered” means every aspect needs a conscious and reasoned decision.","Maturity levels 0–5: Incomplete, Performed, Managed, Established, Predictable, Optimizing; the target is always 3.","In the Results calculation, results above the target are cut back to 3 before averaging, so high and low results cannot compensate each other.","KPI examples are of the types COVERAGE and EFFECTIVENESS and are not mandatory for maturity level 4.","Key changes in ISA 2027: providing policies in 1.1.1 is now a Must, 1.2.4 became 6.1.3, 2.1.3 adds training for senior management, the 6.1.1 requirements were adjusted, and prototype protection was completely rearranged.","The license is CC BY-ND 4.0; derivatives must be marked as not approved by the licensor and all logos and trademarks must be removed."]}]});
